WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.831
As a job for the past 10 years 
and also as&nbsp;recreation, just for fun,

00:00:04.831 --> 00:00:06.840
I create languages.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.945
You know why languages 
die on our planet?

00:00:10.945 --> 00:00:16.201
It's&nbsp;not because government imposes 
one language on a smaller group,

00:00:16.201 --> 00:00:18.672
or because an entire group&nbsp;
of speakers is wiped out.

00:00:18.672 --> 00:00:21.494
That certainly has happened in the 
past, and it's happening now,

00:00:21.494 --> 00:00:23.713
but&nbsp;it's not the main reason.

00:00:23.713 --> 00:00:26.606
The main reason is that 
a child is born to a family

00:00:26.606 --> 00:00:29.998
that speaks a language&nbsp;that is not 
widely spoken in their community,

00:00:29.998 --> 00:00:32.229
and that child doesn't learn it.

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:33.296
Why?

00:00:33.296 --> 00:00:37.584
Because that&nbsp;language is not 
valued in their community.

00:00:37.584 --> 00:00:40.674
Maybe if more people are 
studying more languages,

00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:44.944
it&nbsp;will lead to more linguistic tolerance
 and less linguistic imperialism.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:47.820
Maybe if we actually&nbsp;respect 
language for what it is --

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.142
literally, the greatest invention
in the history&nbsp;of humankind --

00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:54.081
then in the future,

00:00:54.081 --> 00:00:57.796
we can celebrate endangered 
languages as&nbsp;living languages,

00:00:57.796 --> 00:00:59.434
as opposed to museum pieces.

